# Workflow controls and recovery

## Summary
OpenCode registers pause/resume/stop commands and invokes reconciliation on restart.
Recovery observes actual state, reports facts, and waits for explicit resume. Native orchestrator/resume work runs through a visible chat tool; workers and reviewers keep their independent sessions and worktrees.

## Key behavior
- `/ralph-orchestrator` and `/ralph-resume` prompt the main assistant to use `ralph_workflow`. The awaited tool shows worker roles, attempts, selected commands, results and final review reports. No detached top-level workflow runs behind a slash-command acknowledgement.
- A durable question ends the visible step with `waiting_for_reply`. The next real user prompt is captured by OpenCode's prompt hook and bound to requests that already existed; `reply` accepts neither model-supplied answer text nor an approval without a new user message. One message settles at most one request. Replies during an unfinished step do not approve a newly generated proposal.
- Ordinary affirmative replies normalize to the current approval revision. Requested changes re-enter planning and require fresh approval. `/ralph-answer` and its custom TUI dialogs are removed. The TUI only shows scoped status notifications.
- Explicit resume can move an idle feature to the current chat, refreshing unanswered request identities. Active workers prevent concurrent resume; old-session replies cannot answer the new requests. Worker sessions cannot call administrative workflow, removal, setup or control tools. Historical command-verification receipts attached to the main session are excluded from worker classification.
- Interrupting the visible tool stops its owned run, workers and commands using existing cleanup. Progress/result output is bounded and redacts runtime secrets; pending questions and complete worker identities precede logs in the result.
- `/ralph-remove` previews deletion of the checkout's `.ralph` and repository-shared saved workflow state. A new user approval is required. It checks the unchanged preview under setup/write locks and refuses active/unsettled jobs or uncertain live processes. It preserves code, branches and workspaces, including unregistered retained workspace directories. Linked-worktree scope is disclosed. Live host bookkeeping remains while the plugin is loaded, allowing a later setup in the same session.
- Native resume drives the saved phase; it does more than flip the control flag. Pending planning, failed baseline retry, scheduling, and automatic review continuation retain approvals and attempt budgets.
- Before any issue attempts, an explicit `--base` selection after failed baseline cleanup invalidates execution approval and presents the new SHA through the normal chat approval gate. It cannot reset attempts or replace an accepted baseline.
- Pause rejects new jobs and suspends review scheduling while current jobs settle. Production executor submissions retain their attempt identity; mergers and successful in-flight checks wait at the next scheduling/ref boundary rather than becoming failed retries. Each resumed in-flight boundary rechecks the original epoch so stop followed by resume cannot revive a stale callback.
- Stop cancels bound sessions, owned processes and registered environments with bounded cleanup. Late job results cannot advance stopped runs.
- Process ownership requires PID birth identity, owner token, repository and workspace. Owned wrappers watch parent liveness; the dashboard stays in the plugin process.
- Recovery shares the Git common-directory store across worktrees. Already advanced integration intents require exact SHA/check revision evidence and record completion once. Ambiguous operations block resume; validation without output never passes.
- Stop retains existing recovery/cleanup blockers, including repeated stops. Resume cannot race pending stop cleanup. Reconciliation must resolve retained ambiguity.
- Recovery binds production validation to the exact merger envelope, candidate, command hash/revision and approval. It restores the accepted issue result even if the ref or operation result was written before a crash. A proven old index/clean worktree is synchronized with a safe two-tree merge; local changes block recovery.
- Review counters, attempt identities, approvals and artifact revisions are retained; controls do not reset budgets. Explicit resume renews and republishes unanswered main-session questions while preserving recorded answers and remaining active-time budgets.
- Recovery holds a pending control marker through inspection and checks its epoch/revision before publishing. It verifies logical active-run lock identity and the latest accepted refs; uncertain execution/merge/repair completion blocks instead of repeating dispatch.
- An executing attempt with no submission/candidate can be recovered as a consumed failure only after a matching cancelled job, completed abort, idle/missing exact session and proven environment cleanup. Work and attempt history remain available to the next explicit retry. Busy, unknown or mismatched sessions stay blocked.
- Journal locks are replaced only after inspecting their recorded owner birth identity. Missing or corrupt owner evidence does not authorize replacement.

## Code references
- `src/commands/chat-workflow.ts`, `src/core/chat-input.ts`, `src/plugin.ts` — visible workflow steps, real user-message receipts and waiting at chat questions.
- `src/commands/remove-project.ts` — previewed project removal preserving code/branches/worktrees.
- `src/tui.ts`, `tests/integration/tui-input.test.ts` — notification-only TUI without answer dialogs.
- `tests/integration/chat-workflow.test.ts`, `tests/integration/remove-project.test.ts`, `tests/smoke/opencode.test.ts` — chat gates, execution/review progress, cancellation and removal in native OpenCode.
- `src/commands/resume.ts`, `src/plugin.ts` — native resume continuation and guarded starting-commit selection.
- `src/commands/control.ts` — dispatch gates and bounded stop cleanup.
- `src/recovery/reconcile.ts`, `src/state/run-store.ts` — facts, guarded recovered-attempt writes and idempotent operation reconciliation.
- `src/git/worktrees.ts` — safe synchronization after a ref update interrupted checkout synchronization.
- `src/core/orchestrator.ts`, `src/execution/execute-issue.ts`, `src/execution/integrate-issue.ts`, `src/execution/validation.ts`, `src/commands/router.ts` — production pause boundaries and resumed question publication.
- `src/process/ownership.ts`, `src/process/owned-child.ts` — owned process inspection and parent monitoring.
- `src/plugin.ts`, `src/state/host-lease.ts` — filesystem host lease shared across isolated OpenCode location modules; only first activation recovers and last disposal stops work. Another live host fails closed; dead birth identities permit recovery.
- `tests/integration/14-recovery.test.ts` — real Git recovery and lifecycle regressions.

## Related
- [Bounded review repairs](bounded-review-repairs.md)
- [Validation evidence](../../recovery-validation.md)

## Notes
Question/approval persistence and executor/validation recovery have public-fixture coverage. Production input now uses correlated ordinary chat replies, and the automatic review handoff reconstructs its approved lifecycle after explicit resume. Ambiguous publication, cleanup and repair operations stay blocked. Native Linux/Windows process trees and interactive live shutdown still require separate evidence.

## Last updated
2026-10-15

Issue 15 package testing found that a recovery toast awaited during plugin initialization deadlocked the real host. Startup now records reconciliation first and defers the SDK notification to the next event-loop turn; disposal cancels pending notifications. See `src/plugin.ts` and `tests/integration/15-package.test.ts`.

Plugin disposal finishes its host lease even if dashboard shutdown fails. Dashboard shutdown tolerates a listener already closed by OpenCode, preventing a stale closing lease from blocking the next activation.
### 7.11 Stage completion, questions, and attempts

Each stage prompt specifies its completion tool. Ordinary assistant text cannot complete a stage. Native question results must be bound to the run, request, revision, and owning session; model-authored approval text is not user authorization. Duplicate or stale answers must not approve another revision.

Completion calls from a wrong stage, session, or attempt cannot advance state. Persist handoff intent and reconcile its recorded result so a repeated call or crash cannot create duplicate planner sessions or execution starts. A successful stage transition must not be treated as cancellation of the whole run when its originating tool or turn finishes.

PRD authoring and issue planning each have three total attempts: the initial attempt and up to two retries. Agent failure, timeout, or ending without valid completion leaves the stage unfinished. Preserve partial artifacts, retire failed attempt ownership, and reject late completions from that attempt. Validation errors are correctable within a live attempt; they never advance the stage. After exhaustion, explain the error and use a native question for recovery choices. Any user-authorized retry-budget reset is explicit and logged.

If the native question capability is unavailable, report the blocker and keep the request pending. Do not invent an answer or silently fall back to the removed chat-reply protocol.

### 7.12 Controls, recovery, and compatibility

Keep status, pause, resume, stop, and previewed project removal. Pause stops new scheduling while owned operations settle. Stop cancels owned work and preserves recoverable artifacts. Removal preserves project code, Git history, feature branches, and retained workspaces and requires explicit approval under its existing safeguards.

After an interruption during grilling, do not reconstruct or resume the interview from a transcript in a different context. Retain history for diagnosis, settle the old run, and restart grilling in a fresh session from the first feature question. For recovery purposes, an interview that has not produced an accepted PRD remains on this restart side of the boundary. Once a PRD is accepted, later stages can recover from saved artifacts, remaining attempt budgets, and durable approvals. Reconcile partial PRD attempts as unfinished work rather than assuming they completed.

For supported later-stage recovery, inspect sessions, artifacts, commits, processes, and locks before resuming. Do not repeat successful handoffs or integrations or trust interrupted checks as passed. Resume must update control and session binding in a valid order; inactive-state rejection must not be mislabeled as a revision race.

There is no backward compatibility. Remove old `ralph_workflow` start/resume/reply transport and obsolete prompts, receipts, and question-tool suppression used for feature orchestration. Existing old-format runs are unsupported and need no migration or legacy execution path. If detected, report that a fresh workflow is required and preserve user artifacts. This does not remove recovery for runs created by the new workflow.

